{"created": "2026-11-08T14:37:12.191249+00:00", "modified": "2026-11-08T14:37:13.086493+00:00", "id": 24054350, "conceptrecid": "23787052", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.24054350", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.23787052", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24054350", "metadata": {"title": "Two-row covering perfect hash families from Singer cycles, and upper bounds for their length", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.24054350", "publication_date": "2026-11-08", "description": "<p>A covering perfect hash family CPHF(n; k, q, t) is an n \u00d7 k array of points of PG(t\u22121, q) in which every t columns are independent in some row. It yields a covering array CA(n(q^t \u2212 1) + 1; t, k, q), and a CA(n(q^t \u2212 q) + q; t, k, q) when every row misses a hyperplane.</p><p>For every prime power q we give an explicit CPHF(2; 2q+2, q, 4). Its first row consists of two orbits of the third symmetric power of a Singer cycle of PG(1, q) (two twisted cubics), its second row of two orbits of a cyclic group of order q+1 on two conics in a pencil. For every q some member of the family has both rows missing a plane: an explicit one when 3 | q+1; otherwise its existence is proved by counting, for odd q with a Kloosterman-sum estimate over F_{q^3}, and it is found by a search over the planes. Consequently CAN(4, 2q+2, q\u2212j) \u2264 2q^4 \u2212 q \u2212 2j for 0 \u2264 j \u2264 q\u22122.</p><p>At strength five, two orbits of Sym^4 of a Singer cycle in each row give an explicit CPHF(2; 2q+2, q, 5) for every q \u2265 3, so CAN(5, 2q+2, q\u2212j) \u2264 2q^5 \u2212 q \u2212 2j. For v \u2264 25, 52 of the resulting arrays lie below the best known sizes recorded in the ca-tools table on 29 October 2026.</p><p>We then ask how long a CPHF with few rows can be. Let k_n(q, t) be the largest k for which a CPHF(n; k, q, t) exists. Elementary counting gives:</p><ul><li>k_n(q, t) \u2264 (q+1)^n + t \u2212 2, and a sharper recursive bound;</li><li>k_2(q, 3) = q^2 + q + 1 for odd q;</li><li>k_2(q, 4) \u2264 q^2 + 1 for q \u2265 4.</li></ul><p>Equality holds in the last bound if and only if PG(3, q) has an anti-cocircular pair of ovoids, that is, an ACM(2, q) of egglike M\u00f6bius planes in the terminology of Shokri, Moura and Stevens. We prove that no such pair exists for odd q \u2265 7; the known pair for q = 4 gives k_2(4, 4) = 17. For t \u2265 4 the known lower bounds are linear in q, and we ask whether k_2(q, t) = O(q) for odd q.</p><p>Files:</p><ul><li><code>paper_BD.pdf</code> is the paper.</li><li><code>supplement_BD.zip</code> holds the programs, logs and Lean 4 sources (see its README.md).</li><li>The <code>inst_*</code> files are the CNF formulas and LRAT proofs of the three SAT results of Section 9.1, checked with lrat-check.</li><li><code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code> lists the checksums.</li></ul>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Bai, XunDi", "affiliation": "Independent researcher", "orcid": "0009-0004-2432-5034"}], "keywords": ["covering array", "covering perfect hash family", "Singer cycle", "twisted cubic", "normal rational curve", "Kloosterman sum", "Sherwood normal form", "ovoid", "anti-cocircular M\u00f6bius planes", "arc", "MDS code", "MSC 2020: 05B15", "MSC 2020: 05B30", "MSC 2020: 05B40", "MSC 2020: 51E20", "MSC 2020: 51E21", "MSC 2020: 51E22", "MSC 2020: 11T23", "MSC 2020: 94B25", "MSC 2020: 94C12"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23787051", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23787055", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "dataset", "scheme": "doi"}], "version": "2", "language": "eng", "resource_type": {"title": "Preprint", "type": "publication", "subtype": "preprint"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 1, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "23787052"}}]}, "notes": "Version 2 adds the upper bounds for the length of two-row CPHFs (Part B), certified SAT proofs, the Lean 4 formalisation and the supplementary data, and revises the presentation after a referee-style reading. The search, computations, proofs, formalisation and drafting were carried out with Claude (Anthropic) under the direction of the author; see \"Use of AI tools\" in the paper."}, "title": "Two-row covering perfect hash families from Singer cycles, and upper bounds for their length", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/24054350", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/24054350", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/24054350?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24054350", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24054350", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.24054350", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/24054350/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23787052", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/23787052", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23787052", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.23787052", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:24054350/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:24054350/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/24054350/files", "media_files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/24054350/media-files", "thumbnails": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:24054350:paper_BD.pdf/full/%5E10,/0/default.jpg", "50": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:24054350:paper_BD.pdf/full/%5E50,/0/default.jpg", "100": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:24054350:paper_BD.pdf/full/%5E100,/0/default.jpg", "250": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:24054350:paper_BD.pdf/full/%5E250,/0/default.jpg", "750": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:24054350:paper_BD.pdf/full/%5E750,/0/default.jpg", "1200": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:24054350:paper_BD.pdf/full/%5E1200,/0/default.jpg"}, "latest": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/24054350/versions/latest", "latest_html"